open-alert-base @VERSION@
=========================

An open-ALERT base station on an ordinary Debian or Ubuntu computer.

It is the RPi ALERT agent, unchanged from its signed release @TAG@, with the
surroundings a managed network asks for. The agent listens to ALERT and ALERT2
flood-warning radio telemetry on RTL-SDR USB sticks (and on serial receivers),
decodes it, and posts the readings to Flood-Net over outbound HTTPS. Nothing
connects in to it.

NOT YET TESTED WITH A REAL RADIO STICK on a computer that is not a Raspberry
Pi. Everything below was tested with stand-ins for the sticks. Try it on your
own hardware before you rely on it, and run "open-alert-base selftest".

Made for Debian 13 and Ubuntu 24.04, on an ordinary 64-bit PC or a 64-bit ARM
computer. On Debian 12 it installs and runs, but that system's rtl-sdr package
does not know the RTL-SDR Blog V4 stick (V3 and generic sticks only);
"open-alert-base version" says which sticks this computer's rtl-sdr knows.


First steps
-----------

    rpi-alert setup                   the Flood-Net token, a name, the location
    open-alert-base status            is it running, and what does it hear
    sudo open-alert-base audit        the security posture, for a reviewer

The dashboard is a web page the agent serves:
    standard profile     http://<this computer>/        from the local network
    enterprise profile   http://127.0.0.1:8080/         from this computer only
From another computer, anybody can look at it (what is heard, how the base
station is) without a password; changing anything needs its password.
"rpi-alert status" shows the first password, "rpi-alert password" changes it.


The two commands
----------------

    rpi-alert          RPi ALERT's own command, as its documentation describes
                       (rpi-alert help). Three of its commands are for a
                       Raspberry Pi install and are turned away here: daemon,
                       access, import-boot-config.
    open-alert-base    this package's command:
        version        what is installed, and the signed release it came from
        status         the service, the profile, and the agent's own status
        profile [name] show the profile, or put one in force (root)
        sync           make the service and the firewall lock follow the
                       settings as they are now (root)
        audit          print the security posture (root, for all of it)
        selftest       run the agent's own tests on this computer
        bench          measure how many channels this computer will carry


The two profiles
----------------

A profile is a short list of the agent's own settings, merged into
/etc/rpi-alert/config.json. One is applied when the package is first installed
and there is no settings file yet ("standard", unless the install was told
otherwise); an existing settings file is never overwritten by an install.

    sudo open-alert-base profile enterprise

                              standard               enterprise
    dashboard port            80                     8080
    dashboard reached from    the local network      this computer only
    Flood-Net may             change radio settings  only be told how it is
      (remote.mode)           (manage)               (report; "off" tells it nothing)
    own Wi-Fi hotspot         off                    off
    reboots or restarts
      networking by itself    never (off)            never (off)
    screen kiosk, audio       off                    off

"This computer only" is kept by a small table of firewall rules
(/etc/open-alert-base/dashboard-local-only.nft, loaded by
open-alert-base-firewall.service; it needs the nftables package). RPi ALERT up
to 0.23 opens its dashboard on every address of the computer and has no setting
for that; from 0.24 it has one (web.bind), which the enterprise profile already
sets, and the firewall rules stay as a second lock.

To turn remote management off altogether:   rpi-alert remote off

In both profiles the audio and the agent's "self-recovery" are off: the
service is allowed no sound card, and with no program to run as root it could
not restart networking or the computer anyway.

If you change the dashboard's port yourself (rpi-alert config set web.port …),
run "sudo open-alert-base sync" afterwards. A port below 1024 needs one
privilege (CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE), which "sync" and "profile" give the service
in a small file of its own, /etc/systemd/system/open-alert-base.service.d/
10-low-port.conf, and take away again for any other port. Without it the
agent cannot open a low port and falls back to port 8080.


What is where
-------------

    /usr/lib/open-alert-base/agent/     the agent, read-only, as in the signed release
    /usr/share/open-alert-base/         the pin (which release), the release key,
                                        the SHA-256 of every agent file, the profiles
    /etc/rpi-alert/config.json          the agent's settings (they hold the Flood-Net
                                        token: readable by the agent's user only)
    /var/lib/rpi-alert/                 what it has heard and not yet sent, its state
    /etc/open-alert-base/env            a web proxy, a private certificate authority
    /etc/open-alert-base/profile        the profile in force
    /usr/lib/systemd/system/open-alert-base.service
                                        the service: user rpi-alert, no privileges,
                                        the disk read-only but for the two folders above
    /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-open-alert-base.rules
                                        lets the agent's user open the sticks
    /usr/lib/modprobe.d/open-alert-base-blacklist-dvb.conf
                                        keeps the kernel's TV driver off the sticks
    /usr/lib/modules-load.d/open-alert-base.conf
                                        loads the two USB serial drivers at start-up

The log is the system journal:   journalctl -u open-alert-base


A web proxy, a private certificate authority
--------------------------------------------

Put the proxy in /etc/open-alert-base/env (HTTPS_PROXY=…, NO_PROXY=…) and
restart the service. Add a private certificate authority to the operating
system the usual way (update-ca-certificates). The file explains both.


The clock
---------

The agent keeps every reading back until it trusts this computer's clock, so
that none goes to Flood-Net with a wrong time; it sends them, with the right
times, as soon as it does. It trusts the clock once the operating system says
it is set from the network ("System clock synchronized: yes" in what
"timedatectl" prints) or a GPS receiver gives the time. On a network that
blocks the public time servers, point the operating system's time service at
your own. "rpi-alert status" says whether the clock is trusted. For a clock
that is kept right some other way, see RPI_ALERT_ASSUME_CLOCK in
/etc/open-alert-base/env.


What is not here, on purpose
----------------------------

No program the agent can run as root, no sudo rule, no "alert" maintenance
account, no fetching of SSH keys, no updater of its own, no screen kiosk, no
Wi-Fi hotspot, no cap on the system log. Accounts, SSH, operating-system
updates and log collection are your organisation's own. A new version of the
agent arrives as a new version of this package, built from the next signed
release.

With no root helper, some buttons on the dashboard do nothing: restart or
shut down the computer, install an update, join a Wi-Fi network, the hotspot,
SSH access. They answer that the system helper is not installed.


Removing it
-----------

    sudo apt remove open-alert-base     the settings and the data are kept
    sudo apt purge open-alert-base      they are deleted, and the agent's user too


